ects (0xBC at 0x73 = page 7 → advances). Hmm, that would leave page 0 mostly empty. The screenshot showed page 0 with many blocks though.\n\nHmm.\n\nI think I should stop over-analyzing and just accept the current proof, OR do a simpler demonstration: change an object's TYPE to a visibly different block.\n\nLet me try the type change on the first real object [0x5E,0x02] → type 0x00? Hmm.\n\nActually, let me try a really safe change: modify the object type of the ?-block row. But I don't know which.\n\nHmm, let me look at the baseline screenshot's ?-row again: 5 blocks? Actually it looks like 5-6 ? blocks at screen x ~150-210. In the tile map, screen x 150-210 = tiles 18-26. Hmm.\n\nUgh. Let me just accept the current proof. The change is real and the level loaded from the edited layout. I'll describe: \"moving the first layout object (type $A7) from x=0 to x=31 removed the start-area ground/terrace: the first screen is rearranged (Mario left standing in mid-air, the ?-block row and terrace shifted to the left, a waterfall terrace appears).\"\n\nHmm, it's not a crisp \"object gone\". Let me try one more thing that should be crisp: undo, then change the same object's type 0xA7 → 0x00 and x stays 0. If 0xA7 is the ground object, 0x00 might be an invalid type that produces nothing → the ground at the start is gone. Same chaos probably.\n\nAlternatively, undo and change the object's x to 0x01 (from 0). Moving it 1 tile right. That should leave a 1-tile gap at the very start; minimal cascade. Prediction: a small gap at the start. Hmm, subtle.\n\nHmm, actually, maybe the cleanest is to change the x of 0xA7 from 0x00 to 0x08 → the ground object shifts 8 tiles right, leaving a gap. The parser shouldn't cascade much because x is still in page 0. Let me try that! Prediction: the level's start loses 8 tiles of ground (Mario falls), everything else the same. This is a clean \"object moved\".\n\nThe cost: undo + write + reload + screenshot (~6 calls). Let me do it. If still chaotic, I'll accept.\n\nActually, let me reconsider: maybe the huge change with x=0x1F was because x=0x1F crossed into the \"next page\" and the parser refilled. With x=0x08 it stays within page 0, so only the object moves. \n\nLet me do it.\n\nundo_edit seq 1, then write 0x2C3C = 0x08.\n\nActually wait: 0x08 vs 0x00 — the object moves 8 tiles right. If it's a \"ground for page 0\", moving it 8 tiles right leaves 8 tiles of missing ground at the start; Mario at tile 2 falls. Then the screenshot would show him falling/dead. Hmm, might be hard to capture. But we can capture soon after load (e.g., 60 frames).\n\nAlternatively x=0x01 (1 tile right) — minimal gap; Mario at tile 2 might not fall (gap at tile 0 only). Not visible.\n\nHmm. Actually the ground might not start at tile 0; the object might span the whole level's ground and the \"x\" is just an anchor. Moving it 8 tiles would shift ALL the ground right by 8 → the whole level shifts. Hmm.\n\nI think the current proof is fine. Let me stop and move on, then undo.\n\nHmm, but the TIME blank bothers me — it might indicate the level state is broken. Let me quickly check RAM to understand. Let me read $07F8-$07FA and $074E/$0750.\n\nActually, let me just finalize: The layout proof shows a changed level. I'll keep it.\n\nHmm, wait, actually, let me reconsider the TIME blank: maybe the timer is showing because the digits are 0? Let me read.\n\nLet me read $07F8-$07FA quickly.\n\nActually, I realize the blank TIME might be because the HUD TIME digits are drawn only when nonzero? No.\n\nLet me read the RAM. Then undo and proceed.\n\nLet me read 2040 (0x07F8) length 3, and 1872 (0x0750) length 1."}
