{"type":"thinking","text":"Now I understand the enemy format better:\nAt $C144: `LDY $0739` (the enemy data index), `LDA ($E9),Y` reads byte0. CMP #$FF → end. Then `AND #$0F; CMP #$0E` (if low nibble 0x0E → special). Then `CPX #$05; BCC` — X is the enemy slot; if X<5 skip? Then `INY; LDA ($E9),Y; AND #$3F; CMP #$2E`. Hmm.\n\nThen at $C1AB: `LDA $073A` (page) → $6E,X. `LDA ($E9),Y; AND #$F0` → $87,X (x high nibble!). Then `LDA ($E9),Y; AND #$0F; CMP #$0E`. Then at $C1D9: `LDA ($E9),Y; ASL x4` → $CF,X (y). So the byte at index Y (the \"x\" byte?) has: high nibble → enemy x, low nibble → flags/type, and the same byte <<4 → enemy y.\n\nHmm, so actually the enemy record seems to be 2 bytes? byte0 = type (low nibble) + flags, byte1 = position (x high nibble, y derived, low nibble = something).\n\nHmm wait: at $C1AB, `LDA ($E9),Y` with Y = $0739 (current index). Then AND #$F0 → $87,X. Then AND #$0F → CMP #$0E. So the byte at index Y is used both for x (high nibble) and low nibble check. Then at $C1E5, INY, reads the next byte, AND #$40, etc.\n\nSo the record seems to be: byte0 = enemy type (low nibble), byte1 = x/y position? Hmm.\n\nActually, I recall the SMB enemy format now: 2 bytes per enemy! byte0 = enemy type (low nibble) plus flags (high nibble?), byte1 = position (high nibble = x, low nibble = y?). Hmm.\n\nHmm, from $C1D9: `LDA ($E9),Y; ASL A x4` → $CF,X. Here A is the byte at index Y. ASL x4 = <<4, so high nibble becomes high bits. That gives y. So the same byte gives y = (byte << 4). And x = byte & 0xF0. Hmm. So the byte holds x in high nibble and y in low nibble (y = low nibble << 4? no, <<4 makes low nibble → high). So y = (byte & 0x0F) << 4. Hmm.\n\nSo one byte encodes both x (high nibble) and y (low nibble)! And the previous byte encodes the enemy type. So records are 2 bytes: [type, xy]. That matches the length pattern!\n\nLet me re-derive: For 1-2 enemy list starting at $A0D8: records:\n[11, 134], [26, 6], [219, 6], [222, 194], [2, 240], [59, 187], [128, 235