be the decrement routine runs once right at init, turning 401→400 before the HUD is drawn. So displayed 400.\n\nOK so changing table[1] from 4 to 3 → initial 301 → displayed 300. Good, clear difference (400 vs 300).\n\nBut wait, is the timer table really $912D? The code at $9182 is inside a routine that runs at level start (since $0757 is set by the game-start init at $9061). But does it run at EVERY level start or only game start? $0757 is set to 1 at game start ($9063) and cleared by the $9182 routine. So it runs once at game start, not necessarily on every level load! Hmm. But the routine at $9178 is called during area init (the mapping's level-init state machine). Hmm.\n\nActually, the routine at $9172-$91BC contains: STA $03C4, JSR $85F1, then the timer setup, then player setup, then LDY $074E, etc. This looks like the \"InitializeArea for a new game/life\". $0757 being a flag set at game start means it only sets the timer on a fresh game (not on dying/respawning?). Hmm. But SMB resets the timer on each life too. Hmm.\n\nHmm, actually maybe $0757 is set whenever a level is entered. The $9061 routine might be the \"start game\" that sets $0757=1; and after a death, a different path sets it. Hmm.\n\nRegardless, for our proof: we start a brand-new game (reset → title → Start → 1-2), so $0757=1 and the timer is set to table[$0715]. Changing table[1] changes the start time. \n\nOur clean baseline: reset → title → select 1-2 → Start → TIME 400. So the init ran with table[1]=4 → 401 → displayed 400 (or 400 directly). Changing table[1] to 3 should give 300/301.\n\nWait, actually let me double-check $0715 for 1-2. Earlier I read $0715 = 1. Good.\n\nHmm, but if the init sets hundreds=table[$0715] and for 1-2 that's 4, good. Change to 3 → 300.\n\nLet me also consider: maybe table[1] isn't the right index; maybe $0715 for 1-2 is 0? We read $0715 = 1. Good.\n\nSo the timer table is at prgRom 0x112D, entries indexed by $0715, and entry [1] = 4 is what 1-2 uses. I'll change offset 0x112E (table[1]) from 4 to 3.\n\nBut hold on: the task says \"the table that sets the time each level starts with\". The table $912D with values [_,4,3,2] is a 3-entry-ish table giving the hundreds digit (4/3/2 = 400/300/200). Good.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the actual initial timer is 400/300/200 with tens=0, ones=0 and the #$01 is something else. But our test will show.\n\nLet me also double check: does the code set $07FA=1 (ones=1)? If the initial is 401→400, then changing table[1] to 3 gives 301→300. Fine.\n\nLet me proceed:\n1. undo_edit seq 1 (enemy proof).\n2. write prgRom 0x112E = 3, reason.\n3. reset, reach 1-2, screenshot (expect TIME 300).\n\nActually, before undoing, note that the enemy proof and the timer proof could coexist, but to keep clean, undo the enemy edit first.\n\nLet me undo and write.\n\nActually, I could do the timer proof now while the enemy edit is active, but then the level would have no enemies AND timer 300; the TIME change is still the relevant proof. But the task says undo each write once proven. Let me undo the enemy edit first.\n\nLet me do undo_edit seq 1 and write the timer edit.\n\nHmm, wait: after undo, the ledger is empty, and my timer write gets seq 1. Fine.\n\nLet me do undo_edit then write_memory."}
