{"type":"tool_call","callId":"call_02_Oc5X9ErddDrasq7DXBbT9758","tool":"record_mapping","input":{"mapping_type":"note","id":"level timer table","name":"per-level timer setting table","notes":"The table that sets the TIME each level starts with. It lives at CPU $912D = prgRom 0x112D and is 4 bytes: 20 04 03 02. It is read by the level-start routine in the area-init code at CPU $9182 (LDY $0715 / BEQ skip / LDA $0757 / BEQ skip / LDA $912D,Y / STA $07F8), where Y = $0715, the value unpacked from the layout header's byte 0 bits 6-7 by LoadAreaData. The routine then sets $07F9=0 and $07FA=1, so the initial TIME = table[$0715]*100 + 1 (one tick makes it a round value; e.g. 401 -> 400). Index 0 (value $20) is never selected because a zero $0715 makes the code skip (BEQ). Levels with $0715=1 start at 400 (table[1]=4), $0715=2 at 300 (table[2]=3), $0715=3 at 200 (table[3]=2). World 1-1 and World 1-2 both use $0715=1 (their layout headers' top bits are 01). PROOF (ledgered one-byte write + reset + re-reach + undo): wrote prgRom 0x112E (table[1]) 4 -> 3, reset_emulator, re-entered World 1-2 the usual way; the watch over the level load read $07F8=3, $07F9=0, $07FA=1 then 0, and the paused screenshot showed the HUD reading TIME 300 (vs TIME 400 unedited). undo_edit restored 4.","rom_origins":[{"mem":"prgRom","offset":4397,"length":4,"notes":"Per-level timer table at prgRom 0x112D (CPU $912D), bytes 20 04 03 02; entry[1] at 0x112E = 4 is the hundreds digit for levels whose layout-header byte0 bits 6-7 (= $0715) are 1, i.e. World 1-1 and 1-2, giving TIME 400. Entry[2]=3 -> 300, entry[3]=2 -> 200. Read at CPU $9182 during area init."}]}}
